WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Hey, welcome to SpaceX Daily, Episode 30, coming to you from Vancouver.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:10.880
It's July 12, 2026. Plenty moving across Starship, Falcon, and Starlink today.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:17.560
Starships July 16th flight will attempt the first orbital deployment of Starlink V3 satellites,

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:23.760
directly testing the upgraded laser links and mass capacity needed for the next phase of constellation

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:30.420
growth. A Falcon 9 booster completed its 35th flight and deployed 29 Starlink satellites.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:36.380
KeepTrack reports the mission extended the prior 34th flight record on the same hardware

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:42.800
family. The flight supplies fresh data on structural fatigue and life limits while Starship

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.040
development proceeds in parallel. Regular listeners tracking Falcon launch cadence and reuse since the

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:54.300
July 11th episode will note this keeps the annual launch rate ceiling question active.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:00.720
Port photos from July 7th through 9th show standard recovery procedures at the dock with the

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:07.920
transport barge still in position. SpaceX confirmed July 16th as the target date for Starship's

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:14.980
13th integrated flight test. The mission will fly 20 Starlink V3 satellites and test upgraded

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:20.580
laser links, mass capacity, and orbital operations ahead of refueling demonstrations.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:29.380
Report.az and finance.bigo.com both cite the SpaceX site for the explicit July 16th window.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:35.460
Regular listeners will recall the July 11th coverage of ongoing Starbase preparations.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:40.360
This date sets the next concrete milestone for full reuse goals.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:47.260
This marks the first time the upgraded V3 hardware flies on Starship rather than Falcon 9.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:53.600
The official SpaceX page for the 13th flight test outlines the planned orbital deployment sequence

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:00.560
for the initial V3 satellites. India Today notes Starship remains the most powerful rocket scheduled

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:07.080
to fly on that date. Basbranore reports the first Starlink V3 satellites have begun deployment

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:12.200
with improved inter-satellite laser links that increase overall constellation capacity.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:17.660
Early data from the initial units will inform whether the new aperture and power systems

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:24.200
deliver the expected per satellite bandwidth gains. This step directly addresses the open question

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:29.460
of direct to cell ramp and subscriber growth tracked since the July 11th episode.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:35.920
Report.az states that SpaceX confirmed the target date for Starship's 13th

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.100
integrated flight test. The mission will build on the prior campaign by attempting orbital

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:48.180
operations that move the program closer to refueling demonstrations. The announcement names July 16th

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:55.620
explicitly as the window for the first Starlink V3 deployment attempt. Finance.bigo.com adds that

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:02.480
the company set the July 16th window specifically to fly the initial Starlink V3 satellites on Starship.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:08.620
This integrates the two programs by using the larger vehicle to place the upgraded satellites into

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:14.740
orbit. Success here would advance both the Starship reuse timeline and the Starlink capacity

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:20.880
expansion tracked in recent episodes. The target date aligns with the separate SpaceX announcement

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:29.020
naming the same July 16th slot for flight 13. India today coverage confirms Starship flight 13 will

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:35.400
carry 20 Starlink satellites as its primary payload. The vehicle is described as the most powerful

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:42.020
rocket scheduled to fly on that date. This marks the first time the upgraded V3 variant flies aboard

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:48.540
the full Starship stack rather than Falcon 9. The official SpaceX page for the 13th flight test

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:55.160
was released with details on the July 16th target. The page outlines the planned orbital deployment

00:03:55.160 --> 00:04:02.240
sequence for the initial Starlink V3 satellites. This continues the Starship program arc last covered

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:09.400
on July 11th, where preparations at Starbase were noted as the immediate focus. SpaceX entered the

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:16.140
Nasdaq 100 index following the recent IPO and concurrent hardware milestones on both Starship and

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:23.290
Falcon 9. MSN reports index inclusion reflects the combined operational cadence of the two vehicles.

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:30.390
The timing coincides with the 35th Falcon 9 booster flight and the confirmed Starship flight 13

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:38.450
target. SpaceX's SPCX announced the $60 billion acquisition of cursor days after its own IPO,

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:44.970
raising questions about capital allocation and integration complexity. The deal size relative

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:50.190
to recent revenue figures could pressure near-term cash flow if synergies take longer than

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:56.830
expected to materialize. Observers will watch the August float unlock for any early signs of how the

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:03.630
market prices the combined entity. Community members posted that the official Starship flight 13 page

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:10.210
had been released on the SpaceX site. Our slash SpaceX lounge threads quickly filled with users

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:16.070
comparing the listed objectives to prior flight test pages. Several commenters highlighted the

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:21.870
explicit mention of Starlink V3 deployment as the distinguishing element versus earlier flights.

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:28.150
Observers noted Jacqueline remaining in the area while aerial and ground photos documented routine

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:34.470
post-flight activity without new anomalies. One thread examined how the 20 satellite manifest

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:41.430
compares with earlier Starlink payload projections. Our slash SpaceX lounge users shared port pictures

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:48.150
from July 7-9 showing standard booster recovery procedures at the dock along with the transport

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:54.070
barge still holding position. The post received multiple comments discussing visible hardware

00:05:54.070 --> 00:06:00.990
markings without new anomalies. Users discussed the implications of the July 16 target date appearing

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:08.210
simultaneously in multiple independent reports. Participants noted the shift from Falcon 9 to Starship

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:14.850
for the V3 satellites as the key change. Worth keeping an eye on the SI billion dollar acquisition

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:22.330
of cursor announced days after the NASDAQ listing. On the AI front SpaceX purchased cursor for $60

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:29.970
billion shortly after its NASDAQ listing folding the AI code editor into the broader musk compute stack.

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:35.830
The move places GROC models at the center of developer tooling distribution while tying

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:43.190
cursor's engineering resources to SpaceX and XAI infrastructure planning. No further details on

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:49.350
post acquisition roadmap or compute allocation were released in the announcement. The $60 billion

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:55.710
acquisition of cursor was announced days after the NASDAQ listing. From an engineering standpoint,

00:06:55.710 --> 00:07:02.490
the payload bay geometry on Starship Flight 13 must accommodate the V3 satellites new mass and

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:09.290
power budgets while preserving margin for the D orbit burn. The core constraint remains how much

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:14.470
dry mass can be lifted without cutting into propellant reserves needed for the target orbit.

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:21.050
Moving the first V3 satellites onto Starship instead of Falcon 9 tests whether the larger

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:27.010
fairing volume and higher throw capacity can lower the per satellite cost floor that currently

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:34.690
equals raw materials plus solar cells plus laser terminals. If the V3 design scales the aperture

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:41.050
while reusing the phased array architecture, marginal cost per additional bit of throughput drops

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:47.810
once launch price per kilogram falls below the Falcon 9 baseline. The open variable is how many

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:53.330
V3 units fit without exceeding center of mass limits that affect booster return performance.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:08:00.090
Post-flight mass to orbit numbers will show whether satellite side mass reductions or vehicle side

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:07.230
propellant margins need the next redesign cycle. The Falcon 9 boosters 35th flight supplies a live

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:14.230
data set on structural fatigue after repeated thermal cycles and landing loads. Engineers track residual

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:20.690
wall thickness after multiple re entries seal integrity at the inter tank joints and cumulative grid-fin

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:27.030
actuator cycles. Because the vehicle returns successfully the next open measurement is how much margin

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:33.370
remains before the next overhaul or retirement decision. That margin directly informs the annual

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:39.370
launch rate ceiling still listed as an unresolved question for the Falcon program. The decision to

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:45.270
fly the first V3 satellites on Starship also tests integration between satellite bus design

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:52.450
and launch vehicle fairing envelope. V3 satellites carry larger solar arrays and more capable laser

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:59.050
terminals that increase both mass and deployed volume. Starships 9 meter diameter fairing removes

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:04.690
the packaging constraints that forced earlier starlink generations into tighter stowed configurations

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:11.930
on Falcon 9. Post-flight telemetry on satellite separation dynamics and subsequent orbit raising

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:17.570
propellant use will reveal whether the extra volume allows simpler deployment mechanisms or simply

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:26.530
consumes the newly available mass budget. And a quick market note SPC X closed at $145.30

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:34.350
down 4.6%. Before we go Starship's next flight keeps the entire stack moving forward. And that's

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:44.590
a wrap on today's SpaceX developments. SPC X is trading at $145.30 down 4.6%. Share this with a

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:50.190
